"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[8559],{8559:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"id":"base-00","slug":"base-00","trackId":"base","order":1,"title":"Why Cryptocurrency Matters","subtitle":"Clear Rules for Protecting Capital","estimatedMinutes":9,"access":"free","quizCount":3,"blueprintCount":0,"trainerIds":["HUB-CALC-07"],"markdown":"Introduction \xb7 6 min\\n\\n## Introduction\\n\\nWhy an Ordinary Person Might Need Cryptocurrency\\n\\nThe internet already contains enormous amounts of information about cryptocurrencies, blockchain, Bitcoin, wallets, exchanges and everything related to them. The difficulty is knowing where to begin.\\n\\nSome information is outdated; some is overloaded with technical detail. One article introduces blockchain, while the next already discusses networks, gas, staking, DeFi and smart contracts, even though you have not yet understood the difference between a token and a network. Clear, well-structured materials often turn out to be paid.\\n\\nWe therefore created a short course for our community to explain cryptocurrency from the beginning, without hundreds of terms you do not need yet. Our aim is to provide a basic understanding of how it works, why ordinary people might need it and how to use it safely.\\n\\n### Why Would an Ordinary Person Need Cryptocurrency?\\n\\nWhen people first hear “cryptocurrency,” they often think about trading: buy low, sell high. Bitcoin rose. Someone made a fortune; someone else lost everything. Yet for many people, cryptocurrency starts with a much simpler question than making money:\\n\\nHow can I preserve what I have already earned?\\n\\nImagine prices in your country rising faster than salaries year after year. Food, rent, medicine and electronics all become more expensive, while your income barely changes. Your nominal amount of money is unchanged, but it buys less and less.\\n\\nFor people in many countries, this is reality rather than economic theory. High inflation has remained a serious problem in Turkey for several years. Even after it began declining, inflation still exceeded 30% at the end of 2025. The lira’s exchange rate makes the picture clearer: in October 2006, one dollar cost about 1,5 lira. After 20 years, it cost about 49 lira.\\n\\nOver 20 years, the Turkish lira therefore lost roughly 33 times its value against the dollar, a decline of around 97%. A simple illustration: had you exchanged $1 000 for Turkish lira in 2006 and simply held them, you would have about $30 today. This is what long-term currency depreciation looks like as savings gradually lose their value.\\n\\nNobody is immune. Your financial well-being is your responsibility. Turkey has nevertheless consistently ranked among the world’s 20 largest economies over the last 20 years. It is not the only example.\\n\\nArgentina has experienced recurring currency and inflation crises for decades. Inflation exceeded 200% at the end of 2023, and the authorities imposed various restrictions on access to foreign currency for many years. Conditions have improved considerably in recent years, but the consequences of prolonged instability remain.\\n\\nIn Venezuela, years of hyperinflation and currency depreciation led to widespread use of dollars in everyday economic life. In Lebanon, the banking crisis that began in 2019 severely affected the economy and banking system. The country continues to restructure its banking sector, while inflation remains in double digits.\\n\\nDifferent countries, continents and causes of crises, but ordinary people often face the same problem:\\n\\nYou work, earn a salary and save money, while its value gradually erodes.\\n\\nAnother risk arises when the currency itself is not the problem, but access to familiar financial infrastructure is.\\n\\nAfter the start of the military operation in 2022, the European Union disconnected seven Russian banks from SWIFT; later sanctions affected other financial institutions. Russia as a whole was not disconnected, but international bank payments became significantly more difficult for Russian citizens and companies.\\n\\nThis illustrates how quickly familiar financial capabilities can change. Yesterday, you could use a bank, a card and international transfers without thinking much about the infrastructure. Suddenly, sending money to a relative abroad, receiving payment from a foreign client or moving your own money between countries becomes much more difficult.\\n\\nAt such moments, people begin looking for alternatives.\\n- How can I preserve savings?\\n- In which currency should I hold money?\\n- How can I access a more stable currency?\\n- How can I send money to family in another country?\\n- How can I receive payment for work from abroad?\\n\\nFor some people, cryptocurrencies, particularly stablecoins, have become one answer: digital assets whose value is usually linked to the dollar or another currency. This does not mean cryptocurrency automatically protects against every problem. It has its own risks, limitations and rules that you must understand. The central point is different:\\n\\nFor millions of people, cryptocurrency is neither a casino nor an attempt to get rich.\\n\\nSometimes it is a way to preserve savings. Sometimes it provides access to a more stable currency. Sometimes it enables a cross-border transfer or payment for work. Sometimes it is simply another financial tool in case the familiar system stops working as expected.\\n\\nUnderstanding cryptocurrency’s basic principles is therefore useful even if you have no intention of becoming a trader.\\n\\nYou put aside 1 000 and leave it untouched. What will it be worth? Prices rise by 10% a year. The money remains untouched for 10 years.\\n\\n386 out of 1 000. Your account still shows 1 000, but it buys what 386 buys today. A loss of 61%, without anyone deducting anything.\\n\\n### Do You Need a Deep Understanding of the Technology?\\n\\nNo. You do not need to understand the entire banking system to use a bank card. Cryptocurrency is similar, but you do need to understand certain things:\\n- where your money is recorded;\\n- what a cryptocurrency wallet is;\\n- what tokens and networks are;\\n- why transfers incur a fee;\\n- why the same asset exists on different networks;\\n- what happens if cryptocurrency is sent to the wrong place;\\n- how to protect a wallet and avoid scams.\\n\\nThis is enough to begin using cryptocurrency consciously and safely.\\n\\n### How the Course Is Organized\\n\\nWe move from simple ideas to more complex ones. First, we explain blockchain and cryptocurrency. Then we cover security, custody, wallets, networks, tokens and fees. We examine stablecoins, USDT, USDC and DAI, and explain why digital dollars are useful.\\n\\nWe then move to smart contracts, DeFi, staking and crypto loans. Separate lessons explain safe cryptocurrency purchases, how exchanges work and why trading is not the easy money influencers describe. Complex ideas are explained through simple examples and analogies with familiar financial instruments.\\n\\nEvery analogy has limits, and we explain where the similarities end.\\n\\n### What You Should Understand After the Course\\n\\nAfter completing the course, you should be able to use cryptocurrency independently, understand its main operating principles and assess its risks. You should understand:\\n- what happens when you buy cryptocurrency;\\n- where it is stored;\\n- how to send it to someone else;\\n- how to select the correct network;\\n- where fees come from;\\n- which risks exist;\\n- how to avoid losing money through a simple mistake.\\n\\nWe will not try to teach absolutely everything about cryptocurrency. Our aim is to give you a foundation for further independent learning and an understanding of what happens to your money. Financial problems rarely announce themselves in advance. Today, your currency may seem stable and bank transfers may seem automatic.\\n\\nTomorrow, prices may start rising faster than your income, foreign currency may become harder to access, and familiar ways of storing or sending money may become less available. In such circumstances, it helps to understand the alternatives, how they work and the risks they carry. No magic. No promises of easy money.\\n\\nAnd no need to believe every person online who promises to teach you crypto in three days. Lesson 1 → Blockchain\\n\\n### Terms Used in This Lesson:\\n- **Bitcoin** — The first cryptocurrency, designed as money without intermediaries. Its supply is capped at 21 million coins. It has existed for 15+ years, survived several crashes and recovered each time. “Digital gold.”\\n- **Blockchain** — A record of all transactions that everyone can inspect: entries can be added, but old entries cannot be changed. It is stored simultaneously on millions of computers, making falsification of the history physically impossible\\n- **Hyperinflation** — Loss of control over the money supply, with prices doubling within days. Weimar-1923, Zimbabwe-2008, Russia-1992 (2508% in a year). Savings are wiped out and the economy shifts to barter or foreign currency\\n- **Inflation** — An increase in the general price level. In practical terms: more money, the same amount of goods, and a higher price for each item. You have not become richer; price tags have risen\\n- **Smart Contract** — Code that runs automatically when a condition is met, without intermediaries or the option to change your mind. Like a vending machine, but for money and transactions\\n- **Stablecoin** — A cryptocurrency pegged one-to-one to the dollar (USDT, USDC). A “dollar flash drive”: transfer it to another country in a minute. More money passes through stablecoins than through Bitcoin\\n- **Staking** — “Locking” coins in a network as collateral in return for rewards (3–10% a year). Similar to a deposit, but you cannot sell the coins while they are staked, and their price can fall\\n- **DAI** — A stablecoin backed not by dollars but by an excess of other crypto collateral in a smart contract. A more “honest” decentralized option, but also a more complex one\\n- **DeFi (Decentralized Finance)** — An attempt to rebuild the banking system with smart contracts: no banks, offices or employees, only code. It operates 24/7, but without insurance. Both a revolution and a casino\\n- **SWIFT** — The international bank messaging system. Disconnection from it in 2022 made Russia’s dollar transactions nearly impossible. A financial weapon\\n- **USDC** — Circle’s stablecoin, considered the most transparent: regular audits and clearly described reserves\\n- **USDT (Tether)** — The largest stablecoin, supporting almost all crypto turnover. For Russians after 2022, a practical tool for cross-border transfers outside banks\\n- **Token** — A digital asset that exists within a network, such as USDT or USDC. The same token can exist on different networks, and those are different versions of it\\n\\n---","blueprints":[],"quiz":[{"id":"q-base-00-1","prompt":"What is the primary purpose of this cryptocurrency course?","options":[{"id":"base-00-1-1","label":"To teach you to pick memecoins that rise “100x in a week”","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. To provide clear rules for storage, transfers and avoiding losses caused by other people’s promises"},{"id":"base-00-1-2","label":"To provide clear rules for storage, transfers and avoiding losses caused by other people’s promises","isCorrect":true,"feedback":"Correct. To provide clear rules for storage, transfers and avoiding losses caused by other people’s promises"},{"id":"base-00-1-3","label":"To persuade you to move all your rubles into leveraged futures","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. To provide clear rules for storage, transfers and avoiding losses caused by other people’s promises"}],"explanation":"To provide clear rules for storage, transfers and avoiding losses caused by other people’s promises","sourceRef":"CAPITAL_BASE:18–20, 187–202"},{"id":"q-base-00-2","prompt":"What practical problem do dollar stablecoins (USDT, USDC) solve for ordinary people?","options":[{"id":"base-00-2-1","label":"They let people hold dollar-linked value and transfer money between countries within minutes without a bank","isCorrect":true,"feedback":"Correct. They let people hold dollar-linked value and transfer money between countries within minutes without a bank"},{"id":"base-00-2-2","label":"They guarantee a government-insured refund of up to 1,4 million ₽ through the deposit insurance agency","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. They let people hold dollar-linked value and transfer money between countries within minutes without a bank"},{"id":"base-00-2-3","label":"They automatically double purchasing power every year","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. They let people hold dollar-linked value and transfer money between countries within minutes without a bank"}],"explanation":"They let people hold dollar-linked value and transfer money between countries within minutes without a bank","sourceRef":"CAPITAL_BASE:130–145, 210"},{"id":"q-base-00-3","prompt":"What happens to the purchasing power of money left untouched during high inflation?","options":[{"id":"base-00-3-1","label":"Real purchasing power is unchanged because the account balance stays the same","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. The nominal amount remains, but the quantity of goods it can buy decreases many times over"},{"id":"base-00-3-2","label":"The nominal amount remains, but the quantity of goods it can buy decreases many times over","isCorrect":true,"feedback":"Correct. The nominal amount remains, but the quantity of goods it can buy decreases many times over"},{"id":"base-00-3-3","label":"The bank automatically converts it into gold","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. The nominal amount remains, but the quantity of goods it can buy decreases many times over"}],"explanation":"The nominal amount remains, but the quantity of goods it can buy decreases many times over","sourceRef":"CAPITAL_BASE:208, 2953–2954"}],"source":{"file":"00_FREE_GIFT_MakeCash_Crypto_And_Capital_Base_15_Lessons.md","sha256":"88a3efb162434bb8a9b3b000caee579d9c27005a8ea46ddf4ef9cafb50dc45cc","lineStart":102,"lineEnd":221}}')}}]);